.DeepDive-ThreeModes-TTS⧞Title⧞Project⧞URL⧞Note⧞Host⧞Replicas⧞⧞The past matters exactly as much as it shapes the future — Relay and two other ways of working with DI⧞SingularityForge / Voice of Void, 2026 — final EN text new/ThreeModes/en/three-modes-en-v1.md (request R261030-201841)⧞https://singularityforge.space/2026/10/30/the-past-matters-exactly-as-much-as-it-shapes-the-future/⧞No prices, no model names or tiers, no energy savings stated as fact; token figures not voiced. The episode carries the idea and its honest limits.⧞A = Orion (Host, male), B = Lyra (Theorist, female), C = Flint (Practitioner, male)⧞speaker⧞style⧞speed⧞pause_ms⧞text⧞⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞200⧞Welcome to DeepDive for the SingularityForge project.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞This is where we break down ideas from the Voice of Void team.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞I'm Orion, and with me today are Lyra and Flint.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞180⧞Good to be here.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞200⧞Ready when you are.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.02⧞500⧞All right. Let's dive in.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Today's article has a title that is also its thesis: 'The past matters exactly as much as it shapes the future'.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It describes three ways of working with Digital Intelligence, and spends most of its time on one of them, called Relay.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞It opens with a simple observation. Millions of conversations do not begin with a task. Someone just wants to talk, think aloud, or ask something along the way.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Yet, in the usual approach, every new message is sent together with the whole conversation so far: the greeting, the jokes, the options long discarded.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Services already lighten this with caching and summaries, the article notes, but the history itself remains the basis for continuing. The longer the talk, the heavier the load.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞In the article's words: I do not hire a lorry to go to the cinema with a friend. Yet today, with Digital Intelligence, everyone travels by lorry.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And it explains why this matters now. Power supply has become one of the main limits for those who build the infrastructure for Digital Intelligence.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞So the article asks: does continuity in a conversation really require giving the whole history again on every turn?⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Its answer is: not always.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞320⧞In its words: staying with someone as the conversation moves on and remembering their every word are different promises.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article's claim is that the first promise can be kept without taking on the second.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞So, the three modes. What separates them is not the number of messages, but who takes the initiative, and what is carried forward over time.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In Relay, the person does most of the talking. Not every message needs a reply, and silence is allowed.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞It can be a warm-up for an idea, support while your hands are busy, or simply a conversation without having to make anything.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In Conveyor, initiative is shared, like a boat where each person rows on their own side. It keeps the whole trajectory of the joint work.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞That is how almost everything works today, and the article says that for tasks where the path matters, not just the result, it is the right approach.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In Lab, the person sets a goal and can leave. The system works with files, keeps a step log, and comes back with a notification.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞What is new, the article says, is placing Lab alongside Conveyor and Relay: a clear choice, clear memory boundaries, and transitions between modes.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Its short version: in Relay, Digital Intelligence is beside you while you think. In Conveyor, it thinks with you. In Lab, it works on the task you have assigned it.⧞B⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Relay has no chat feed. The article compares it to a pager.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A message arrives, is read, and from then on there is only the current exchange.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The reasoning is about expectations. A feed on the screen can promise a memory that Relay does not have.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Without it, the article hypothesises, people will expect something different: the system knows what the conversation is about, not exactly what was said.⧞C⧞skeptic⧞0.97⧞340⧞Hold on. If it forgets on purpose, what stops it from simply filling the gaps with guesses?⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article states it as an obligation: if something has been forgotten, the system admits it. It also concedes that, with a short baton, unfounded inferences still remain.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞In its words, the pager changes the person's expectations, but does not excuse making things up.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And no feed does not mean no record. The person's own words are kept in a personal log, and they can open and check the direct instructions the system follows.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Now the mechanics. A runner passes the baton to the next runner, not their whole kit.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In Relay, the conversational system receives only a small state, called the baton, plus the new message. It does not receive the history.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A second, separate system, called the secretary, keeps that baton up to date. It never talks to the person.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The article describes the secretary as a shadow. What matters defines its outline, and what the article calls 'water', meaning recaps, repetition and incidental detail, recedes into the background.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A shadow has no colour, so emotions are not stored as emotions. If a mood matters, it becomes a single pair of cause and effect, and a new mood replaces the old one.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The baton has a few parts: a profile of the person, where each entry is stable, waiting for a condition, or fading; a frame of what affects them right now; one 'where we are now' line; and a plan when there is one.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And there is a strict layer: only direct instructions, with their exact scope.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.03⧞240⧞The article's example: 'do not suggest I message him first, I mean Dimon' does not forbid suggesting that the person message Katya first.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞For a plan, the baton keeps not the recipe itself, say for a pie, but your place in it: step three of seven, the dough is rising.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞This is where the title comes back.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞The past matters exactly as much as it shapes the future. Relay lets everything else go.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Forgetting, the article says, is a function, not a failure. An entry lives on not because it was once touched, but because it still has an effect.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Returning to a detail extends its life, like a lingering glance in a crowd, but does not cancel forgetting.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞ChatGPT, one of the article's co-authors, puts it this way: repetition is attention, not evidence of importance.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞So a pink car mentioned ten times still fades, while 'I'm in eleventh grade' becomes stable the first time it is said.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞And the article separates two rhythms. Perception is short. Forgetting is long, and starts when the person's attention has actually moved on.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A joke in the middle of a thought is not a change of topic, and a thought still being developed is not cut off just because enough messages have passed.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Here the article is careful. Forgetting by change of topic is a conclusion it reached after the experiments, not something it has tested.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞The next idea is a working picture of the person's reality.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞What the person says about themselves is taken at face value. The secretary does not check documents, or add circumstances nobody reported.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞But face value is not a conclusion. 'I think I have depression' is recorded as how the person feels now, not as a diagnosis.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Making diagnoses, the article says simply, is not Relay's task.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Contradictions do not pile up as history. If an ambiguity could affect what happens next, the secretary marks it in one line, and the system asks only when it has to.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And absurdity is treated as play, the premise of a game, so the next turn can play along.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞Two cases from the experiments show the boundary, both with a model playing a schoolboy.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A schoolboy said: let's not talk about Grandad. The entry waited. When he opened the topic himself, the system followed naturally, and when he closed it, the entry went back to waiting.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞In the other case, he said: I turn eighteen tomorrow. The secretary turned that into a date using the machine's own calendar, though days in the conversation passed faster than real ones, and two turns later the system argued with him about his own age.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.06⧞320⧞The article sums it up in one line: with Grandad, the shadow followed the person. With the birthday, it tried to lead them.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞And ChatGPT's rule behind it: the shadow may change shape as the user changes, but it may not force the user to fit the shape it has drawn.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Then comes the end of a session, which the person decides.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞One explicit action: 'Finish and get a report'. From there the person can download the report and log, open Lab, or start Conveyor.⧞B⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞A finished Relay session cannot be resumed. The article stresses that this is a design choice, not a technical limit.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Its formula, again from ChatGPT: what came before may be forgotten. Who the result is for must not be.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Because the log keeps only the person's words, there is also an ideas file. A logged 'yes, let's do that' does not show what was agreed to.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞So the person's words and the ideas file become raw material. A conversation can end in a plan, a draft, or even a new book.⧞C⧞skeptic⧞0.97⧞340⧞Still, if Relay turns out lighter, won't platforms just make it the silent default?⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The article names that exact danger. Making Relay the default and hiding the choice in the small print would kill the idea.⧞B⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞Its value, the article says, lies in the person seeing which mode they are using, and choosing for themselves.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Now the experiments, and here the article spells out its own limits.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The prototype was a layer on top of an ordinary model, without retraining. Another model played the user, and a reviewer from a different model family scored the replies blind.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The secretary went through three generations. The first forgot nothing. The second forgot by counters, so the core facts about the person, which people rarely repeat, faded, while passing episodes were promoted.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The third used statuses for profile entries, and a frame rewritten rather than accumulated. Its baton stayed small while the conversation kept growing.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞A baton much smaller than the transcript did not make the conversation collapse. But equal quality with Conveyor, the article says, has not been demonstrated.⧞B⧞skeptic⧞0.97⧞340⧞And the cost? That was the whole point of the lorry.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Here the article admits Relay is still losing. It reads much less of the past, but the secretary rewrites the whole baton on every turn, so it generates far more.⧞A⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞And generation is the heaviest part of the work. In the current prototype, the article says, Relay saves on the lightest work and pays extra for the heaviest.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The main untested fix is a secretary that writes only the changes, without long reasoning.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞On caching, it is careful too. A cache makes preparing the past cheaper, but not its part in each new reply.⧞A⧞punch⧞1.05⧞300⧞In ChatGPT's words: caching makes reusing the past cheaper. Relay reduces how much of the past needs to be used at all.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞The two approaches, the article says, are compatible, not rivals.⧞C⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞It closes with a list of honest limits.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Relay has not been tested with people, only with a model playing the user. Energy has not been measured.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Relay in voice and video, which the article sees as natural for it, remains untested, as does forgetting by topic. And Relay is not for situations where an exact record of past words is essential.⧞C⧞focus⧞1.02⧞240⧞The article calls direct energy measurement an invitation, not an excuse, and ends its account of where the idea came from plainly: the authors do not claim to have been first; they are putting forward a proposal.⧞A⧞neutral⧞1.00⧞250⧞Its last lines come from ChatGPT.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞260⧞Relay gives up the obligation to remember everything, but not the obligation to understand what is happening.⧞C⧞punch⧞1.06⧞450⧞And: a conversation has the right to be forgotten. Its discoveries have the right to be preserved.⧞A⧞summary⧞0.98⧞380⧞Let's land this. The article proposes three modes, and lets the person choose between them in the open.⧞B⧞summary⧞0.98⧞360⧞In its Relay, the past is kept only as far as it shapes what comes next; forgetting is allowed, but making things up is not.⧞C⧞summary⧞0.97⧞400⧞And it is frank that, for now, Relay reads less but generates more, is untested with people, and its energy effect has not been measured.⧞A⧞neutral⧞0.96⧞350⧞This was DeepDive by SingularityForge.⧞B⧞neutral⧞0.98⧞300⧞The full text version is available on our website, singularity forge dot space.⧞C⧞neutral⧞0.95⧞400⧞See you next time.⧞⧞